// A constant wrapper: a function whose whole result is one scalar literal,
// computed from nothing. `ch_nl() { } return int 10;` is the constant 10 under
// a function's name -- a veiled reference to a constant, which a reader has to
// open to learn that it never changes. A constant is declared as one, in
// conf.id, and read with (import ch_nl); so a function that only wraps one is
// a compile error.
//
// It sits beside the duplicate checks because it is the same complaint about
// the same thing: one value, spelled as something it is not.
//
// Values are the fingerprint canonicaliser's (../../../../canon/). A literal,
// or an operator over literals that canon_bin's constant fold already reduces,
// fingerprints as I<n>, F<spelling> or S<spelling>; nothing else does. So the
// rule can miss a wrapper the fold does not reduce, but it cannot reject a
// function whose result depends on anything but literals.

cw_scan() {
  int i = 0;
  while(i < len((import prog))) {
    cw_at(i);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return void;

cw_at(int i) {
  int id = (import prog)[i];
  string val = cw_value(id);
  if(cw_is_lit(val) == 1) {
    cw_report(id, val);
  }
} return void;

// "" unless the function is a constant wrapper, else the constant's
// fingerprint.
cw_value(int id) {
  string val = "";
  if(cw_shape(id) == 1) {
    val = cw_body(id);
  }
} return string val;
